<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giotto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1267–<a href="/years/1337">1337</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Giotto di Bondone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giotto di Bondone was a Florentine painter and architect whose naturalistic figures and sense of space broke with the Byzantine style and laid foundations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painting. His major works include the frescoes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel">Scrovegni Chapel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> of around 1305 and the Bardi and Peruzzi chapels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a> and designed Florence's campanile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giotto.jpg" alt="Giotto" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto,_Santa_Maria_Novella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almost every aspect of Giotto's life is subject to controversy: his birth date, birthplace, apprenticeship and the order of his works. Tradition places his birth in a farmhouse near Colle di Romagnano, but recent research has presented documentary evidence that he was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the son of a blacksmith named Bondone. Giorgio Vasari tells that the painter Cimabue found him as a shepherd boy drawing his sheep on a rock and took him as an apprentice. Many scholars today consider this a legend and cite earlier sources suggesting he was not Cimabue's pupil. Around 1290 he married Ricevuta di Lapo del Pela, and they had four daughters and four sons, one of whom, Francesco, became a painter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Giotto worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1297-1300. By 1301 he owned a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and documents of 1301 and 1304 show large estates there; he probably led a large workshop with commissions from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Around 1305 he painted the interior frescoes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel">Scrovegni Chapel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, commissioned by Enrico degli Scrovegni. Its 37 scenes follow the lives of Joachim and Anne, the Virgin and Christ in three tiers, with the Last Judgement on the west wall and monochrome figures of the virtues and vices below. His figures are solidly three-dimensional, with faces and gestures based on close observation and garments that hang naturally, unlike the stylized figures of Cimabue and Duccio.</p><p>Whether he painted the Life of St Francis in the Upper Church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a> is among the most disputed questions in art history; technical comparisons in 2002 gave strong evidence that he did not. He painted frescoes in the Lower Church there between 1306 and 1311. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> he painted the Ognissanti Madonna, the only panel painting universally accepted as his, and the Bardi and Peruzzi chapels in Santa Croce, whose dates are disputed. The Peruzzi cycle was especially admired in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> times, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaccio">Masaccio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> are known to have studied it. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert of Anjou</a> called him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he stayed until 1333.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1334 the Commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> appointed Giotto chief architect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>, and he designed the bell tower, begun on 18 July 1334. After his death in January 1337 Andrea Pisano and then Francesco Talenti completed it in 1359, not entirely to his design. His contemporaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> and Giovanni Villani praised him, and Vasari credited him with breaking from the Byzantine style. Vasari says he was buried in Florence Cathedral. Bones found in the 1970s beneath Santa Reparata were examined in 2000 and judged by Italian researchers to be his, while others, among them Franklin Toker, are sceptical.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giotto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished at court and in the capital, from <b>Giotto</b> and Petrarch under Robert the Wise to the humanists of Alfonso V, the painting of Caravaggio and Luca Giordano, and the Neapolitan school of opera in the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Giotto di Bondone</b> transformed painting in the early fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards the commune flourished: the basilica of Saint Anthony was built, <b>Giotto</b> painted the Scrovegni Chapel around 1305, and the scholar Albertino Mussato revived classical Latin drama.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-padua">Commune of Padua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vicchio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.93,11.47&amp;year=1312" aria-label="Vicchio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4825 608 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giotto"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5275 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5275 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.93,11.47&amp;year=1312">What was here in 1312? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
